    "evidence_text": "The twin-arginine translocation (Tat) protein export pathway\n\nTAL effectors are remote controls for gene activation.\n\nPAAR-repeat proteins sharpen and diversify the Type VI secretion system spike\n\nPAPERFLICK:\n\nGram-negative bacteria use the Type VI secretion system (T6SS) to inject toxic proteins into rival bacteria or eukaryotic cells.\n\nHowever, the mechanism of the T6SS is incompletely understood.\n\nTssK was confirmed to be essential for effector secretion by the T6SS.\n\nMembrane topology of the 60-kDa Oxa1p homologue from Escherichia coli.\n\nHaemolysin coregulated protein is an exported receptor and chaperone of type VI secretion substrates.\n\nIdentification of T6SS-dependent effector and immunity proteins by Tn-seq in Vibrio cholerae.\n\nType VI protein secretion system (T6SS) is important for bacterial competition through contact-dependent killing of competitors.\n\nT6SS delivers effectors to neighboring cells and corresponding antagonistic proteins confer immunity against effectors that are delivered by sister cells.\n\nA view to a kill: the bacterial type VI secretion system.\n\nThe contracted organelle is then recycled in an ATP-dependent process.",
